sceneDescription:
Fiona — an adult woman with light brown straight neck-length hair brushed back with a center part, blue eyes, a soft jawline, wearing a white linen pirate shirt with full sleeves gathered at the wrist and a laced V-neckline, a black felt tricorn hat with a shallow brim worn back from the face, a brown leather wide belt with a large square brass buckle, deep blue linen knee-length breeches, and brown leather calf-high folded-cuff boots, a silver necklace with a heart pendant at the neckline and a silver bracelet on her left wrist — stands at the stone balustrade of the Quaibrücke, facing the camera, both hands gripping the Schatzkarte open wide, the cream-coloured paper with its left half dense with crisp dark-brown coastlines and compass rose and its right half a pale faded wash held up at chest height. The wide grey stone pedestrian bridge stretches behind her, its low stone balustrade running along both sides, the deep blue-green lake water below catching full Hochsommer light and a pale blue summer sky overhead. Directly below at the low quay landing tied to the mooring point at the bridge's edge sits the Goldene Möwe — the dark brown wooden hull sitting low in the water, its single mast rising from the centre of the deck, its cream-white square sail with the black crossed-bones shape furled on the horizontal yard, bow blunt and rounded, dressed with a triangle of additional bunting along the rope railings to pass as a pirate vessel. Facundo — a young adult man with short tousled dark brown wavy hair, brown eyes, an angular jawline and a clean-shaven face, wearing an orange linen pirate shirt with full sleeves rolled to the elbow and a laced V-neckline, a black leather wide belt with a large square brass buckle, charcoal grey linen knee-length breeches, and black leather calf-high folded-cuff boots, a beaded necklace at the neckline — lies tangled in the rope railing near the Goldene Möwe's stern, eyes closed, his body slumped and limbs loose in sleep, his kit heaped beside him with the wooden oar — a pale natural-wood shaft roughly twice a person's height, its broad oval blade angled upward at a diagonal — sticking out of the pile. Two mute swans, large fully white waterbirds with orange-red bills and black knobs at the base, wings slightly raised as they push off, long curved necks extended forward, slide away from the blunt rounded bow and move out onto the open water. Across the blue-green lake the Fraumünster shows as a dark silhouette — a Gothic church with a steeply pitched dark slate-grey roof and a single slender tapering spire rising from the roof ridge — above pale stone walls. Wide establishing shot, bright high-summer midday light, open sky.

